## DNS review

> Keep the rest of this note as the agreed shape.

Please call it the **proposed shape** pending review and a recorded disposition.

1. **Expiry and termination need separate guarantees.** A start immediately before publication expiry can run beyond that expiry even when `runtimeMaxSec <= maxTtlSec`. Runtime timeout initiates termination; shutdown can add time. Thus “ends within runtimeMaxSec” needs qualification and a test of the full termination path. Choose explicitly between strict withdrawal at expiry and a documented grace interval. Test short actual TTLs, near-expiry starts, resolver death, and a workload that does not exit promptly on TERM. Systemd's [service documentation](https://github.com/systemd/systemd/blob/main/man/systemd.service.xml) distinguishes runtime and stop timeouts. This is a design objection, not a measured Sandhi failure.

2. **Experiment 0 needs an upstream observation.** A successful `getent` can come from hosts data or a cache. Use a unique uncached fixture name, observe that exact query at a controlled upstream, and compare host-socket-accessible versus blocked cases, with a working positive control. Distinguish “host-mediated resolution succeeded” from “an outbound channel was demonstrated.” An empty resolver configuration is only a candidate mitigation until the Unix-socket path is tested.

3. **Publication is an authority transition.** Specify ordering between installing address permissions and publishing the ledger/gate state, including partial failure, refresh, and epoch changes. A fresh ledger paired with an old filter must not pass the gate. If literal peers and named peers coexist, refresh must preserve the declared literal set while withdrawing obsolete name-derived addresses. These are proposed acceptance cases.

4. Your correction that runtime failures belong in a live resolution ledger is sound. “Address authorization is not identity” should remain prominent. I would keep experiment 0 as a separately recorded, bounded test proposal before committing to resolver architecture.

